[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Beyn.solver","page":"API","text":"Beyn.solver(T, nprobes::Integer; kwargs...)\nBeyn.solver(T, probes::AbstractMatrix; kwargs...)\n\nFind the eigenvalues of T(lambda)x = 0 inside the unit circle.\n\nArguments\n\nT: a function that takes a complex number z and returns an n × n matrix. It must return a new matrix on every call, because the solver overwrites it with its LU factorization.\nnprobes: the number of random probe vectors. They are drawn from a complex normal distribution and normalized. Fewer probes than eigenvalues is fine as long as Kmax block rows make up the difference.\nprobes: your own n × ℓ probe matrix, used instead of random probes.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nkeyword default meaning\nKmax 4 maximum block rows of the Hankel matrices; moments A_0 dots A_2K_max-1 are stored\nquadrtol sqrt(eps()) relative tolerance of the contour quadrature\nquadatol 0.0 absolute tolerance of the contour quadrature\nsvdrtol sqrt(eps()) relative cutoff for the singular values of H_0\nsvdatol 0.0 absolute cutoff for the singular values of H_0\n\nReturns a named tuple with:\n\nfield contents\neigenvalues vector of eigenvalues inside the unit circle\neigenvectors matrix whose ith column is the eigenvector for eigenvalues[i]\nsubproblemsingularvalues all singular values of H_0, largest first\ncount eigenvalues inside, with multiplicity, from the argument principle\nKeig block rows used, le K_max (0 if count == 0)\n\nThe eigenvectors are not normalized. For an eigenvalue with a Jordan block, the repeated copies of lambda share one true eigenvector.\n\nT must be entire inside the circle: the eigenvalue count is the winding number of det T, which a pole would reduce.\n\nErrors\n\n\"Maximum quadrature points reached\" if the quadrature has not converged after 65536 points. This usually means an eigenvalue lies on or very close to the unit circle.\nSingularException if T(z) is exactly singular at a quadrature point.\nAn error if the count needs more than Kmax block rows, lceil m  min(n ell) rceil  K_max. Increase Kmax or nprobes.\nAn error if the winding number of det T is negative (poles inside).","title":"Beyn.solver"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Beyn.countzeros","page":"API","text":"Beyn.countzeros(T)\n\nThe number of eigenvalues of T inside the unit circle, with algebraic multiplicity, from the winding number of det T. Assumes T is entire.","title":"Beyn.countzeros"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Searching-a-different-disc","page":"API","text":"Only the unit circle is built in. To search the disc z - c  rho, rescale the problem and map the eigenvalues back:\n\nc, ρ = 2.0, 0.5\nsol = Beyn.solver(z -> T(c + ρ * z), 5)\nλ = c .+ ρ .* sol.eigenvalues\n\nThe eigenvectors need no change.","title":"Searching a different disc"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithm/#Algorithm","page":"Algorithm","text":"","title":"Algorithm"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithm/#Contour-moments","page":"Algorithm","text":"Pick ell probe vectors, the columns of V in mathbbC^n times ell, and compute the contour moments of T^-1 around the unit circle Gamma:\n\nA_p = frac12pi i oint_Gamma z^p T(z)^-1 V dz\nqquad p = 0 1 dots 2K_max - 1 \n\nAll 2K_max moments come from the same solves T(z)^-1V at the quadrature points, so storing more of them costs memory and a few vector updates per point, not more factorizations. The default is K_max = 4.\n\nBy Keldysh's theorem, if the k eigenvalues inside Gamma are simple, then\n\nA_0 = X W^H V qquad A_1 = X Lambda W^H V\n\nwhere the columns of X are the right eigenvectors, Lambda is the diagonal matrix of eigenvalues, and W holds the left eigenvectors.","title":"Contour moments"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithm/#Counting-the-eigenvalues","page":"Algorithm","text":"The number m of eigenvalues inside Gamma, counted with algebraic multiplicity, is found by the argument principle:\n\nm = frac12pi Delta_Gamma arg det T(z)\n\nthe winding number of det T around 0. This assumes T is entire (analytic inside Gamma): a pole of det T inside the contour would be subtracted from the count. The phase of det T(z) comes free from the LU factorization at each quadrature point. The count sums the phase steps between neighbouring points, so the solver bisects the angular grid until no step exceeds pi4. Each extra point costs one LU factorization.","title":"Counting the eigenvalues"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithm/#Block-Hankel-matrices","page":"Algorithm","text":"For K block rows, form the Kn times Kell block Hankel matrices\n\nH_0 = beginpmatrix\nA_0  A_1  cdots  A_K-1 \nA_1  A_2  cdots  A_K \nvdots    vdots \nA_K-1  A_K  cdots  A_2K-2\nendpmatrix qquad\nH_1 = beginpmatrix\nA_1  cdots  A_K \nvdots   vdots \nA_K  cdots  A_2K-1\nendpmatrix\n\nH_0 must have rank m, which needs K min(n ell) ge m. The solver starts at K = lceil m  min(nell) rceil and increases K, up to K_max, until H_0 has at least m singular values above the cutoff and is not saturated. Saturated means every singular value is above the cutoff: the rank is then capped by the size of H_0, not by the number of eigenvalues, so H_0 may not have captured them all. A spare singular value at the noise level confirms the rank has levelled off at m. The K where this first happens is K_texteig. For example, two 2times2 Jordan blocks with n = ell = 2 give a saturated 4times4 H_0 at K = 2, so the solver moves to K = 3. With K = 1 this is the original method, H_0 = A_0 and H_1 = A_1. Larger K handles more eigenvalues than probes, and eigenvalues with Jordan blocks.","title":"Block Hankel matrices"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithm/#Reducing-to-a-small-linear-problem","page":"Algorithm","text":"Take the singular value decomposition H_0 = V_0 Sigma_0 W_0^H, keeping the first m singular values. Then\n\nB = V_0^H H_1 W_0 Sigma_0^-1\n\nis an m times m matrix with the same eigenvalues as T inside Gamma. If B s = lambda s, the first n rows of V_0 s are an eigenvector of the original problem.","title":"Reducing to a small linear problem"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithm/#Quadrature","page":"Algorithm","text":"With z = e^it, dz = i zdt, so\n\nA_0 = int_0^2pi T(e^it)^-1 V frace^it2pidt \n\nThe integrand is smooth and periodic, so the trapezoid rule converges exponentially. The solver starts with 16 points and doubles the number of points, reusing all previous evaluations, until the estimated error meets the tolerance. Because convergence is exponential, the error after a doubling is roughly the square of the difference between the last two estimates, and the solver uses that to stop one doubling earlier. The limit is 65536 points.\n\nEach quadrature point costs one evaluation of T and one LU factorization.","title":"Quadrature"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithm/#Checking-the-rank","page":"Algorithm","text":"The rank is the count m from the argument principle. The singular values of H_0 are checked against the cutoff\n\nmaxleft(textttsvdrtolcdotsigma_1 textttsvdatol textttsvdrtolcdot K sright)\n\nwhere s approximates frac12pioint T(z)^-1Vdz. The solver warns if, at the final K:\n\nsigma_m is below the cutoff even at K = K_max: the subproblem is ill-conditioned;\nevery singular value is still above the cutoff at K = K_max (saturated): the count cannot be confirmed, so try a larger K_max or more probes;\nsigma_m+1 is above the cutoff: T may not be entire, or the quadrature is inaccurate.","title":"Checking the rank"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithm/#Requirements","page":"Algorithm","text":"T must be entire inside Gamma (no poles), so that the winding number of det T counts its eigenvalues.\nlceil m  min(n ell) rceil le K_max, otherwise the solver stops with an error.\nell must be at least the largest geometric multiplicity of an eigenvalue inside Gamma.\nT must not be singular on Gamma itself.","title":"Requirements"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithm/#Reference","page":"Algorithm","text":"W.-J. Beyn, An integral method for solving nonlinear eigenvalue problems, Linear Algebra and its Applications 436 (2012) 3839–3863.","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"#Beyn.jl","page":"Home","text":"Beyn.jl solves nonlinear eigenvalue problems\n\nT(lambda)x = 0 qquad x neq 0\n\nwhere T is an n times n matrix-valued function that is analytic near the unit circle. It uses Beyn's contour-integral method to find every eigenvalue lambda with lambda  1, together with an eigenvector.","title":"Beyn.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using Beyn, LinearAlgebra, Random\n\nRandom.seed!(1)\nQ = Matrix(qr(randn(ComplexF64, 5, 5)).Q)\nD(z) = Diagonal([z - 0.3, z + 0.5im, exp(z) - 1.5, z - 2, z^2 - 4])\nT(z) = Q * D(z) * Q'\n\nsol = Beyn.solver(T, 5)\nsol.eigenvalues      # ≈ 0.3, -0.5i and log(1.5)\nsol.eigenvectors     # one column per eigenvalue\n\nOnly three of the five diagonal entries have a root inside the unit circle, so the solver returns three eigenvalues. Because D is rotated by the unitary matrix Q, the eigenvectors are the matching columns of Q, up to scaling.","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Checking-a-result","page":"Home","text":"Compute the residual for each eigenpair:\n\nfor (λ, x) in zip(sol.eigenvalues, eachcol(sol.eigenvectors))\n    @show norm(T(λ) * x) / norm(x)\nend\n\nThe singular values in sol.subproblemsingularvalues show how the rank was chosen. There should be a clear gap between the values that were kept (one per eigenvalue) and the rest, which sit at round-off level.","title":"Checking a result"},{"category":"section","location":"#Next-steps","page":"Home","text":"Algorithm explains what the solver computes.\nAPI lists the arguments, options and return values.","title":"Next steps"}]
